<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Habsburg Netherlands</h1><p class="st-meta">1482 CE – 1556 CE · capital Mechelen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Low Countries under Maximilian, Philip the Handsome and Charles V, gathered into the Seventeen Provinces and bound to the Habsburg world empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy's</a> death in 1482 left the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>'s northern lands to her infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a>, with her widower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Austria</a> as regent. Maximilian fought rebellious Flemish towns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for a decade before Philip came of age in 1494.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1500, rounded off the territories by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-utrecht">Utrecht</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-overijssel">Overijssel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a>, forming the Seventeen Provinces. Regents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-austria-duchess-of-savoy">Margaret of Austria</a> and Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> governed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mechelen">Mechelen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-general-of-the-netherlands">States General</a> and collateral councils. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe's</a> main market, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> worked in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-louvain">Leuven</a>. The Pragmatic Sanction of 1549 made the provinces one indivisible inheritance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> abdicated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1555, handing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a>. Philip's rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> began the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a>, and his religious and fiscal policies provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/habsburg-netherlands.jpg" alt="Habsburg Netherlands" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1482 CE – 1556 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mechelen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maximilian I, Philip the Handsome, Charles V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seventeen Provinces, Burgundian Circle</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1519&amp;focus=habsburg-netherlands" aria-label="Habsburg Netherlands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc